On Sat, 27 Jul 2002 21 Crispin Cowan wrote:

> This results in an odd conclusion: anyone can distribute SELinux-derived
> works under the terms of the GPL (provided they're willing to risk SCC
> later changing their mind) *except for SCC*. Because SCC's Statement of
> Assurance is in violation of the GPL, SCC is specifically enjoined from
> releasing modified linux kernels based on this restricted use of their
> patent. If SCC ever wants to distribute SELinux based products, they are
> going to have to actually GPL the patent, as opposed to this "assurance"
> document.
> 
> Crispin


SCC did distribute the code to the NSA. At that point in time
SELinux became GPL. Under the GPL the NSA release SELinux to 
the general community.

Does anybody not see that when SCC was paid ( A lot of
money) to build SELinux and delivered it to the NSA at 
that point in time SELinux became GPL. They built a 
product by modifing Linux (which is GPL) and sold it 
to the NSA. 

I guess this is what Microsoft refers to as "Infectiuos"
licensing. There lawers are smart enough to know when
you modify GPL code to build a product then the product
is GPL. By no means am I a supporter of Microsoft, but
rather a strong supported of Open Source.

SELinux is GPL and can be royalty-free distributed 
under the GPL license. The GPL does make the SCC patent 
GPL, but all code based on SELinux is GPL. In other 
words you cannot not build a new product using new 
code and based on the SCC patent and sell it without 
licensing the patent from SCC.

Mark

